nodes.append({
 "id": 4, "lang": "Avestan", "words": "baraziš, barašnav",
 "verdict": "plausible",
 "derivation": "The first word is right and is the core of the whole branch: Av. barəziš- n. 'Polster, Kissen' (Bartholomae AiW col. 950, with the Vendīdād passages), = Skt. barhis- 'sacrificial straw, litter', and Bartholomae himself adds 'np. bālis Kissen. — Pü.: bālisn'. Pokorny puts precisely this pair under bhelǵh- at p.125-126: 'bhelgh- schwellen; Balg (aufgeblasene Tierhaut), Kissen, Polster (Erw. von bhel- aufblasen usw.). Ai. barhiš n. Streu, Opferstreu = av. barəziš- n. Polster, Kissen, npers. bālis Kissen'. The second word, barašnav, is Av. barəšnav-/barəšnu-, and it does NOT mean 'cushion, pillow': Bartholomae (col. 951) glosses it 'Höhe, Scheitel' ('height, crown of the head'), the word behind the Zoroastrian baršnūm purification (MacKenzie: 'baršnūm, the major purification ritual'). Hübschmann is Nourai's actual source for the pairing: 'bālis Kissen, phl. bālisn ..., zd. barəzis; phl. bālīn a summit, the crown of the head ..., zd. barəšnu- Höhe, Gipfel (aus *barz-nu-)'. So Nourai has silently carried the gloss 'pillow' over from the first word to the second, and *barz-nu- belongs rather with *bherǵh- 'high'. Compatible picture, imprecise gloss — hence 'plausible'.",
